For the integral [latex]
\mathrm{I}=\int_{-1}^{1} \frac{1}{\mathrm{x}^{2}} \mathrm{dx}
[/latex] which of the following statements is TRUE?

The following function is defined over the interval [latex][-\mathrm{L}, \mathrm{L}][/latex] : [latex]
f(x)=p x^{4}+q x^{5}
[/latex] If it is expressed as a Fourier series, [latex]
f(x)=a_{0}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left\{a_{n} \sin \left(\frac{\pi x}{L}\right)+b_{n} \cos \left(\frac{\pi x}{L}\right)\right\} \text {, }
[/latex] which options amongst the following are true?

Cholesky decomposition is carried out on the following square matrix [latex][\mathrm{A}][/latex]. [latex]
[A]=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
8 & -5 \\
-5 & a_{22}
\end{array}\right]
[/latex] Let [latex]\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{ij}}[/latex] and [latex]\mathrm{a}_{\mathrm{ij}}[/latex] be the [latex](i, j)^{\text {th }}[/latex] elements of matrices [latex][L][/latex] and [latex][A][/latex], respectively. If the element [latex]I_{22}[/latex] of the decomposed lower triangular matrix [latex][\mathrm{L}][/latex] is 1.968 , what is the value (rounded off to the nearest integer) of the element [latex]a_{22}[/latex] ?

The theoretical aerobic oxidation of biomass [latex](\mathrm{C} 5 \mathrm{H} 7 \mathrm{O} 2 \mathrm{~N}[/latex] ) is given below: [latex]
\mathrm{C}_{5} \mathrm{H}_{7} \mathrm{O}_{2} \mathrm{~N}+5 \mathrm{O}_{2} \rightarrow 5 \mathrm{CO}_{2}+\mathrm{NH}_{3}+2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}
[/latex] The biochemical oxidation of biomass is assumed as a first-order reaction with a rate constant of [latex]0.23 / \mathrm{d}[/latex] at [latex]20^{\circ} \mathrm{C}[/latex] (logarithm to base e). Neglecting the second-stage oxygen demand from its biochemical oxidation, the ratio of [latex]\mathrm{BOD} 5[/latex] at [latex]20^{\circ} \mathrm{C}[/latex] to total organic carbon (TOC) of biomass is (round off to two decimal places). [Consider the atomic weights of [latex]\mathrm{C}, \mathrm{H}, \mathrm{O}[/latex] and [latex]\mathrm{N}[/latex] as [latex]12 \mathrm{~g} / \mathrm{mol}, 1 \mathrm{~g} / \mathrm{mol}, 16 \mathrm{~g} / \mathrm{mol}[/latex] and [latex]14 \mathrm{~g} / \mathrm{mol}[/latex], respectively]

